<+>%       File: VTthesis_template.tex\r\n%     Created: Thu Mar 24 11:00 AM 2016 EDT\r\n%     Last Change: August 15, 2024\r\n%     Author: Alan M. Lattimer, VT\r\n%\t  With modifications by Carrie Cross, Robert Browder, and LianTze Lim. \r\n%\r\n% This template is designed to operate with XeLaTeX.\r\n%\r\n% All elements in the Title, Abstract, and Keywords MUST be formatted as text and NOT as math.\r\n%\r\n%Further instructions for using this template are embedded in the document. Additionally, there are comments at the end of the file that give suggestions on writing your thesis.  \r\n%\r\n%In addition to the standard formatting options, the following options are defined for the VTthesis class: proposal, prelim, doublespace, draft. \r\n\r\n% Today: \r\n% Solvers\r\n% Tommoroq: \r\n\r\n\r\n% Sections Remaining: \r\n\r\n% Introduction\r\n\r\n% Literature Review\r\n\r\n% Nomenclature\r\n\r\n% Nick Newton citation\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n% Overall Archeture Description \r\n\r\n% Mass analysis FEA \r\n\r\n% Stress and shape functions\r\n\r\n% Solvers (Done ish)\r\n\r\n% Results\r\n\r\n%\t% Main Solution\r\n\t% Sensitivity Analysis\r\n\t% Design Space Plots \r\n\t\r\n\r\n\r\n\\documentclass[doublespace,nopageskip]{VTthesis} % nopageskip - Removes arbitrary blank pages.\r\n%Remove \"draft\" from \\documentclass to remove draft watermark and to display figures you've created.\r\n\r\n% Using the following header instead will create a draft copy of your thesis\r\n%\\documentclass[doublespace,draft]{VTthesis}\r\n\r\n% The lipsum package is just included to put dummy text in the document in order to demonstrate page headers and table of contents behavior. You should remove it once you begin writing your actual thesis or dissertation.\r\n\\usepackage{lipsum}\r\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\r\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\r\n\\usepackage{siunitx}\r\n\r\n% Title of your thesis\r\n\\title{Optimization of a UAV for Wildfire Management}\r\n\r\n% You should include 3-5 keywords, separated by commas\r\n\\keywords{Some Keywords, Subject matter, etc.}\r\n\r\n% Your name, including middle initial(s)\r\n\\author{Nathaniel S. Hargan}\r\n\r\n% Change this to your program, e.g. Physics, Civil Engineering, etc.\r\n\\program{Mechanical Engineering} \r\n\r\n% Change this to your degree, e.g. Master of Science, Master of Art, etc.\r\n\\degree{Master of Science} \r\n\r\n% This should be your defense date:\r\n\\submitdate{December XYZ, 2025} \r\n\r\n% Committee members. Only have five readers and one chair available.\r\n% Only use the ones you need and don't include the ones you don't need.\r\n% You can also declare a Co-advisor. Per the VT ETD standards, \r\n% you should not include titles such as Dr. or Professor, but can list educational \r\n% qualifications after the name (i.e., John Doe, MFA or Jane Smith, PhD)\r\n% Names should appear as they do in ESS/Banner/HokieSpa, including middle initials.\r\n\\principaladvisor{Robert L. West Jr., Ph.D.}\r\n%\\coadvisor{Vicente Esparza}\r\n\\firstreader{Michael Philen}\r\n\\secondreader{Pınar Acar, Ph.D}\r\n% \\thirdreader{XYZ}\r\n%\\fourthreader{Fourth Committee Member}\r\n%\\fifthreader{Fifth Committee Member}\r\n\r\n% The dedication and acknowledgement pages are optional. Comment them out to remove them.\r\n% \\dedication{This is where you put your dedications.}\r\n% \\acknowledge{This is where you put your acknowledgments.}\r\n\r\n% The abstract is required.\r\n\\abstract{Give a brief description of your thesis here.}\r\n%\\abstract{\\lipsum [1-4]}\r\n\r\n% The general audience abstract is required. There are currently no word limits.\r\n\\abstractgenaud{You are also required as of Spring 2016 to include a general audience abstract. This should be geared towards individuals outside of your field that may be reading seeking information about your work. You should avoid language that is particular to your field and clearly define any terms that may have special meaning in your discipline.}\r\n\r\n\r\n\\begin{document}\r\n\t% The following lines set up the front matter of your thesis or dissertation and are required to ensure proper formatting per the VT ETD standards. \r\n\t\\frontmatter\r\n\t\\maketitle\r\n\t\\tableofcontents\r\n\t\r\n\t% The list of figures and tables are now optional per the official ETD standards.  Unless you have a very good reason for removing them, you should leave these lists in the document. Comment them out to remove them.\r\n\t\\listoffigures\r\n\t\\listoftables\r\n\t\\printnomenclature %Creates a list of abbreviations. Comment out to remove it. \r\n\t\r\n\t% sample text for abbreviations:\r\n\tNLP is a field of computer science, artificial intelligence, and linguistics concerned with the interactions between computers and human (natural) languages.\r\n\t \r\n\t\\nomenclature{NLP}{Natural Language Processing}\r\n\t\r\n\t$\\sigma$ is the eighteenth letter of the Greek alphabet, and carries the 's' sound. In the system of Greek numerals, it has a value of 200. \r\n\t\r\n\t\\nomenclature{$\\sigma$}{The total mass of angels per unit area}\r\n\t\r\n\t% The following sets up the document for the main part of the thesis or dissertation. Do not comment out or remove this line.\r\n\t\\mainmatter\r\n\t\r\n\t%now go ahead and start writing your thesis\r\n\t\\chapter{Introduction} \\label{ch:introduction}\r\n\t\r\n\t%Copy/paste the code below to add sections and subsections to each chapter. Add your own text to the chapter and (sub)section labels to create custom headings.          \r\n\t\\section{One Section} \\label{se:one_section}\r\n\t\\subsection{A sub-section} \\label{ss:this_subsection}\r\n\t\\section{Another Section} \\label{se:another_section}\r\n\t\r\n\t\\chapter{Review of Literature} \\label{ch:lit_review}\r\n\t\r\n\t\\chapter{UAV Design} \\label{ch:uav_design}\r\n\t\r\n\t\tThis chapter describes the UAV geometry, design variables, material properties, and design parameters used in the project. The mission profile and the thrust, power, and energy formulations used to evaluate vehicle performance are also described. \r\n\t\r\n\t\\section{UAV Geometry} \\label{se:uav_geo}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe baseline UAV configuration used in this study follows a previously developed firefighting UAV design \\cite{nick}. The UAV is modeled as a simplified octocopter frame, as shown in Figures \\ref{fig:arm}–\\ref{fig:hub}, with individual frame components highlighted. The frame consists of three primary components: arms, struts, and a central hub. The arms and struts are modeled as beam elements with annular cross sections. The hub is composed of two octagonal plates connected by a radially symmetric web, shown in Figure \\ref{fig:hubsec}, consisting of eight web sections spanning from the center to the edges of the plates. All connections between arms, struts, and hub are modeled as fully rigid.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.015]{Images/arm.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Optimized UAV Model with Highlighted Arm}\r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:arm}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.015]{Images/strut.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Optimized UAV Model with Highlighted Strut} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:strut}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.015]{Images/hub.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Optimized UAV Model with Highlighted Strut} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:hub}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}[h]{0.45\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.01]{Images/hubtop.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Top Section View} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{sfig:hubsectop}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\\hfill\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}[h]{0.45\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.01]{Images/hubsectioniso.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Isometric View} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{sfig:hubseciso}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Optimized UAV Model Hub Cross-Section View}\r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:hubsec}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\\subsection{Design Variables} \\label{ss:design_variables}\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\t% Dimensions and Design variables\r\n\t\tEight design variables were selected for this analysis and are shown in Figure \\ref{fig:dv_vis}. The eight arms of the UAV frame are defined by the arm outer diameter ($d_a$) and arm wall thickness ($t_a$) design variables. The design variables associated with the struts are the strut outer diameter ($d_s$), strut wall thickness ($t_s$), and the distance from the center of the UAV to the ends of the struts ($r_s$). The design variables associated with the hub are the flange thickness ($t_f$), the hub web thickness ($t_w$), and the hub radius ($r_h$).  \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[!htbp]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}{0.48\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=0.85\\linewidth,keepaspectratio]{Images/Dv/da.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Arm Diameter}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_da}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\\hfill\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}{0.48\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=0.85\\linewidth,keepaspectratio]{Images/Dv/ra.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Arm Thickness}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_ta}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\vspace{3pt}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}{0.48\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=0.85\\linewidth,keepaspectratio]{Images/Dv/ds.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Strut Diameter}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_ds}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\\hfill\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}{0.48\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=0.85\\linewidth,keepaspectratio]{Images/Dv/ts.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Strut Thickness}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_ts}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\vspace{3pt}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}{0.48\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=0.85\\linewidth,keepaspectratio]{Images/Dv/rs.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Strut Distance from Center}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_rs}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\\hfill\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}{0.48\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=0.85\\linewidth,keepaspectratio]{Images/Dv/tf.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Hub Flange Thickness}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_tf}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\vspace{3pt}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}{0.48\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=0.85\\linewidth,keepaspectratio]{Images/Dv/tw.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Hub Web Thickness}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_tw}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\\hfill\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}{0.48\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=0.85\\linewidth,keepaspectratio]{Images/Dv/rh.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Hub Radius}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_th}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Design Variable Visualizations on UAV model}\r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:dv_vis}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t% # https://genstattu.com/tattu-40000mah-6s-10c-22-8v-high-voltage-uav-lipo-battery-pack-with-as150-as150-plug/?srsltid=AfmBOoqN5rYTguPKErrR2RGJXuesTt9UqwIoJ9aieuGaF-ipxM2Kz1VW\r\n\t\\subsection{Design Parameters}\t\\label{ss:des_para}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t% Geometric Design Variablees\r\n\t\r\n\t\tThe wingspan and hub height are two parameters of the UAV frame that were not selected as design variables. The inner hub height is set to 64mm, which represents the minimum height required to fit the proposed battery between the two plates. It should be noted that the actual batteries would not fit on this frame model, because the hub web would intersect the battery. Further work could be done to refine the mesh, allowing for more realistic battery storage. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe wingspan was set as a parameter due to the fact that increasing beyond the minimum will not increase the objective function, as increasing the wingspan increases mass and the mass moment of inertia while also causing more stress on the vehicle. The distance from the center of the UAV to the propeller is calculated to be 1127.3mm. This is calculated by finding the minimum distance required to fit all 8 propellers with a radius of 406.4mm with a propeller spacing of 25mm. \r\n\t\r\n\t\t% (self.parameters[\"propeller_spacing\"] + self.parameters[\"propeller_radius\"]) / math.sin(\r\n\t\t% math.pi / self.parameters[\"blade_num\"])\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\\subsection{Mass and Payload}\t\\label{ss:payload}\r\n\t\r\n\t\tThe UAV carries a payload of 45 kg, which is located at the center of the vehicle. Figure \\ref{fig:fbs} shows free-body diagrams (FBDs) of the UAV system. Figure \\ref{sfig:fbdmot} illustrates the FBD of the UAV during acceleration. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tFor the finite element analysis system presented in later chapters, a static system is required, so a stress-equivalent system was constructed, as shown in Figure \\ref{sfig:fbdstat}. This model assumes that the entire mass of the frame is concentrated at the vehicle’s center. This method overestimates the stress in the frame due to all the mass being modeled at a single point. However, the mass of the frame is minimal compared to the mass of the payload so this should have little effect.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe stress-equivalent force ($F_\\text{equivalent}$) in each propeller for the UAV in hover is $m_\\text{total}g/8$ where $m_\\text{total}$ is the total mass of the UAV, $g$ is gravity. The stress-equivalent force for the UAV with drag and acceleration considered over the duration is discussed in Subsection \\ref{ss:thrust}.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}[h]{0.45\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{Images/FBDMotion.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Accelerating UAV} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{sfig:fbdmot}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\\hfill\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{subfigure}[h]{0.45\\textwidth}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{Images/FBDStatic.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{Static Stress-Equivalent} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{sfig:fbdstat}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{subfigure}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Free-Body Diagrams of Simplified 2D UAV Representation}\r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:fbs}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\r\n\t\\subsection{Material Properties}\t\r\n\t\t% Material Propeeties\r\n\t\tThe material selected for all UAV frame components (struts, arms, and hub beams) is a braided carbon fiber composite. For the purposes of this project, the carbon fiber is modeled as isotropic. Future work could include obtaining more accurate material data to model the carbon fiber as anisotropic. The properties listed in Table \\ref{tab:matprop} correspond to data obtained from ALLRed Corporation for carbon fiber braided round tubes.\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{table}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Material Properties for Braided Round Tubes from ALLRed Corporation \\cite{dragonplate:private}} \r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=1]{Tables/Matprop.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\label{tab:matprop}\r\n\t\t\\end{table}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe shear strength is calculated from the relationship tensile strength and poisons ratio between the shear strength. Due to lack of fatigue information for the specific carbon fiber composites, fatigue material data is taken from Aluminum7075-T6. \\cite{MatWeb} The fatigue strength coefficient ($f'$) for Aluminum7075-T6 is 1466 MPa. The fatigue strength exponent ($b$) is -0.143. The fatigue strength cycles is 1e6 ($N$). The completely reversed stress amplitude was calculated from the equation $\\sigma_{rev} = f'(2N)^b$.\t\r\n\t\t% All data for materials is stored within the Material dictionary within the material{\\_}properties module. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tA Woehler curve for the fatigue analysis as created using the Pylife Library. \\cite{pyLife} The completely reversed stress amplitude is input as 'SD', the fatigue strength cycles for 'ND', and the fatigue strength exponent for 'k{\\_}1'. % This was used for the WoehlerCurve class. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% https://store.tmotor.com/product/mf3218-polymer-folding-prop.html\r\n\t\t\t% [Please Ask Dr. West to Send Motor Link] (I lost it)\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\t- The UAV is modeled as a simplified version of a UAV frame \r\n\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t[Talk about the briefly original UAV design (I do not know where to find this)\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t- Frame designed from 3 components\r\n\t\t\t- Arms\r\n\t\t\t\t- Annulus \r\n\t\t\t\t- Design variables are radius, thickness\r\n\t\t\t\t- Arm length runs from the end of the hub to the wingspan\r\n\t\t\t\t- the wingspan is the minimum required to fit all propellers with 25 mm of spacing\r\n\t\t\t- Struts\r\n\t\t\t\t- Annulus\r\n\t\t\t\t- Design variables are radius, thickness, and distance from center \r\n\t\t\t- Hub \r\n\t\t\t\t- Two octagonal plates with sides perpendicular to the arm\r\n\t\t\t\t- the web runs in between each \r\n\t\r\n\t\t\t\t- Flange thickness, web thickness, and Hub radius are design variables.\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t- They are assumed to be welded together \r\n\t\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t- Payload of the UAV is 45 kg\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t- Material Properties\r\n\t\t\t- Table of the relevant material properties of dragon plate. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t- Cross Section Properties\r\n\t\t\t- Table of the relevant cross section properties\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\t- Arm length is assumed to be the minimum allowable length that allows for 50mm of spacing between propellers.\r\n\t\t \t \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t- Hub height is determined to be 64mm to allow for clearance of the battery. [battery source]\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\end{comment}\r\n\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\\section{Mission Profile} \\label{se:mission_profile}\r\n\t\r\n\t\tA model of the mission profile was created to represent a potential mission with continuous position, velocity, and acceleration as functions of time. The mission is defined as a series of connected profile segments, with constraints imposed on position, velocity, and acceleration at the nodes between segments. Any unconstrained nodal values are calculated using basic kinematic relationships. Between segment nodes, position, velocity, and acceleration are interpolated using cubic Hermite splines, resulting in acceleration that varies linearly with respect to time.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe mission profile is based on a potential operation during the 2025 Palisades wildfire. The total mission distance is estimated to be 5600m, corresponding to the distance from the northern edge to the southern edge of the active fire area. Using elevation maps of the surrounding terrain, the required altitude change to traverse this path is estimated to be 460m \\cite{MapViewer}. Timesteps were selected such that the maximum downrange velocity remained below 12m/s.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe climb, cruise, and descent phases are each described using two cubic Hermite spline intervals to avoid over-constraining the mission profile. Figure \\ref{fig:xvy} shows altitude as a function of downrange position, Figure \\ref{fig:yvt} shows altitude versus time, and Figure \\ref{fig:xvt} shows downrange position with respect to time. The constraints applied to the mission profile are summarized in Table \\ref{tab:profcons}, where the duration represents the time between spline intervals. Blank entries indicate that no constraint was applied.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{Images/MissionProfile/XvY.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Downrange Position vs. Altitude}\r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:xvy}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{Images/MissionProfile/YposvT.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Altitude vs. Time} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:yvt}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{Images/MissionProfile/XposVT.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Downrange position vs. Time} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:xvt}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\r\n\t\t\\begin{table}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Mission Profile Constraints} \r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.7]{Tables/profcons.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\label{tab:profcons}\r\n\t\t\\end{table}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\t% My specific Architecture\r\n\t\t% The design flow diagram for the mission profile generation process is shown in figure \\ref{fig:dfd_mission}. Mission profiles are generated in the mission{\\_}profile module. This module contains a MissionProfile class, MissionProfileSegment class, and the kinematic{\\_}matrix function. The list of nodes is added by the add{\\_}segment method within the MissionProfile class. The MissionProfile contains a list of constraints added by the add{\\_}constraint method. The add{\\_}constraint requires the node at which the constraint will be applied, the type of constraint (x, y, x-velocity, y-velocity, x-acceleration, y-acceleration), the value of the constraint. To solve for the unknown node values, the solve{\\_}kinamatics method within the MissionProfile class assembles matrices generated from the kinematic{\\_}matrix function into a kinematic matrix for the entire mission profile. The remaining position, velocity, and acceleration values at the nodes are then calculated using the numpy.linalg.lstsq function. The solution at the nodes are applied with the time{\\_}solve method which uses a cubic Hermite spline to find interpolate values at a given time. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe design flow diagram for the mission profile generation process is shown in figure \\ref{fig:dfd_mission}. The constraints require the node at which the constraint will be applied, the type of constraint (x, y, x-velocity, y-velocity, x-acceleration, y-acceleration), and the value of the constraint. The constraints are then assembled into a kinematic matrix for the entire mission profile. Each node refers to a specific time set by the user. The values at the nodes are solved using the numpy.linalg.lstsq function. The values between nodes are interpolated with a cubic Hermite spline.\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.05]{Images/DFDs/DFDMissionProfile.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Mission Profile Generation Process} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_mission}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\\subsection{Thrust Calculation}\\label{ss:thrust}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tA calculation of the thrust profile is required in order to preform the power and fatigue analysis. This is the stress-equivalent force over the duration of the mission profile with drag and acceleration taken into account. The thrust equation over the duration of the mission profile is shown in equation \\ref{e:thrust}. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t\tF_{\\text{thrust}}(t)\r\n\t\t\t=\r\n\t\t\t\\pm v(t)^2 \\, s_a \\, \\rho \\, c_d\r\n\t\t\t+\r\n\t\t\tm\\bigl(a(t)+g\\bigr)\r\n\t\t\t\\label{eq:thrust}\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe v(t) and a(t) represent the velocity and acceleration of the mission profile at given point in time. $s_a$ is an estimate of the surface area of UAV. This is obtained by summing the projected surface of every beam and hub segment with the projected surface area of the two batteries. The surface area is a function of the design variables. $\\rho = 1.2250\\unit{\\kilogram\\per\\meter\\cubed}$ is the air density. $c_d = 0.256$ is the drag coefficient. $g = 9.8066\\unit{\\meter\\second\\squared}$ was used for the gravity constant. The mass is estimated as the sum of 45kg payload, the mass of the frame, and the mass of the two batteries.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tTo simulate the pre-liftoff and post-landing conditions, a rev-up and rev-down period were added to the thrust forcing function. The ramp-up phase is modeled as a linear, increasing thrust from zero to the thrust required for takeoff, while the ramp-down phase is modeled as a linear decrease in thrust to zero. The rev down period was modeled as a linearly decreasing force until no force is applied. A duration of five seconds is used for both periods.  \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.6]{Images/ThrustVTime.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Thrust vs. Time Plot} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:thrust}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\\section{Energy Consumption} \\label{se:energy_consumpt}\r\n\t\t% \tThe energy consumption of the UAV over the mission profile is calculated for the energy constraint. The design flow diagram for this energy consumption algorithm is shown in Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_mission}. Within the DronePower class, the thrust to power conversion and energy consumption calculations are performed. Once the thrust function is obtained, as described in Subsection \\ref{ss:thrust}, it is passed to the thrust to power conversion model described in Subsection \\ref{ss:power_calc}. This model computes the power at each time step. The resulting power profile is then integrated to determine the total energy consumption, as discussed in Subsection \\ref{ss:energy_consumption_calc}. This calculation requires the total energy capacity of the battery system.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe energy consumption of the UAV over the mission profile is calculated for the energy constraint. The design flow diagram for this energy consumption algorithm is shown in Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_mission}. Once the thrust function is obtained, as described in Subsection \\ref{ss:thrust}, it is passed to the thrust to power conversion model described in Subsection \\ref{ss:power_calc}. This model computes the power at each time step. The resulting power profile is then integrated to determine the total energy consumption, as discussed in Subsection \\ref{ss:energy_consumption_calc}. This calculation requires the total energy capacity of the battery system.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{Images/DFDs/DFDEnergy.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Energy Consumption Calculation} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_encon}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Batteries} \\label{ss:batteries}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t% The battery used is the Tattu HV 40000mAh 10C 6S1P 22.8V High Voltage UAV Lipo Battery Pack \\cite{GensTattu}. Two of these batteries are connected in series, resulting in a total system voltage of 45.6 V. The energy capacity ($E_{Capacity}$) of the battery system is calculated by multiplying the total system voltage by the amp-hours of the battery. Battery power data and related calculation are stored within the BatterySystem class within the drone{\\_}power module. The batteries have the dimensions of 263x122x64mm. The centroid of the batteries are positioned at (0, 86.75, 0) and (0, -86.75, 0) in the global coordinate system. % The mass and position data for the batteries are stored within the Battery class within the drone{\\_}battery module.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe battery used is the Tattu HV 40000mAh 10C 6S1P 22.8V High Voltage UAV Lipo Battery Pack \\cite{GensTattu}. Two of these batteries are connected in series, resulting in a total system voltage of 45.6 V. The energy capacity ($E_{Capacity}$) of the battery system is calculated by multiplying the total system voltage by the amp-hours of the battery. The batteries have the dimensions of 263x122x64mm. The centroid of the batteries are positioned at (0, 86.75, 0) and (0, -86.75, 0) in the global coordinate system. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Power Calculation} \\label{ss:power_calc}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\tThe motor used for vehicle propulsion is the U8 Lite L Efficiency Multirotor UAV Motor KV95 \\cite{Motor}. The propeller used was the MF3218 \\cite{Propeller}. Test data from the supplier shown in Table \\ref{tab:power}for the thrust against the power, and RPM for the pair of motor and propeller was used to generate Equation \\ref{eq:t_to_p} and Equation \\ref{eq:ang}. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.7]{Tables/motortab.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Test Data for 8 Lite L Efficiency Multirotor UAV Motor KV95 with MF3218 Propeller \\cite{Motor}} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{tab:power}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tSo that the power can be found at any point along the curve, the quadratic function of best fit was found for thrust vs power data. Equation \\ref{eq:t_to_p} shows the resulting thrust to power relationship. Figure \\ref{fig:power} is a plot of this data relevant to the equation. $P(t)$ is the power over time in units of watts. $T(t)$ is thrust in units of grams. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation} \\label{eq:t_to_p}\r\n\t\t\tP(t) = 1.1743 \\times 10^{-5}\\, T(t)^2\r\n\t\t\t+ 4.2302 \\times 10^{-2}\\, T(t)\r\n\t\t\t+ 1.5871 \\times 10\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.7]{Images/ThrustVPower.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Thrust Vs. Power curve for the 8 Lite L Efficiency Multirotor UAV Motor KV95 with MF3218 Propeller \\cite{Motor}} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:power}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Angular Velocity Calculation} \\label{ss:ang_calculation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tFor the perturbation model and frequency constraint, the angular speed of the propeller over the duration of the mission ($\\omega(t)$) was calculated. Equation \\ref{eq:ang} was created by finding the quadratic function of best fit of the RPM vs Thrust data from the supplier's test data. This equation converts the thrust ($T(t)$) in units of grams into angular velocity in units of rotations per minute. Table \\ref{tab:angu} contains the line of best fit as well as the motor data. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation} \\label{eq:ang}\r\n\t\t\t\\omega(t) = -9.3523 \\times 10^{-6}\\, T(t)^2\r\n\t\t\t+ 3.1211 \\times 10^{-1}\\, T(t)\r\n\t\t\t+ 8.0573 \\times 10^{2}\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.7]{Images/ThrustVRPM.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Thrust Vs. Angular Velocity Curve for the 8 Lite L Efficiency Multirotor UAV Motor KV95 with MF3218 Propeller \\cite{Motor}} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{tab:angu}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Energy Consumption Calculation} \\label{ss:energy_consumption_calc}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe energy consumed over the mission duration is computed by subtracting the time integral of the power draw from the total energy capacity of the battery system. Equation \\ref{eq:p_to_e} describes the relationship between the energy remaining in the battery and the power consumed. $E_{Remaining}(t)$ is the total energy remaining between both batteries as a function of time. $E_{Capacity}$ is the energy stored by a fully charged battery. $t$ is the time since the start of the mission.  \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation} \\label{eq:p_to_e}\r\n\t\t\tE_{\\text{Remaining}}(t) = E_{\\text{Capacity}} - \\int_{0}^{t} P(t) \\, dt\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe integral of the power is calculated numerically using scipy.integrate.cumulative{\\_}trapezoid. The number of time steps was is a number set when running the optimization function. The total span of time calculated is the duration of the mission.  \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\\subsection{Perturbation Calculation} \\label{ss:purtibation}\r\n\t\r\n\t\tFor the transient damage analysis a model of oscillations caused by propeller perturbations was also required. This as modeled as a force over time. The desired function is a sinusoidal wave with a frequency matching the propeller frequency. The amplitude was estimated to be 1\\% of the thrust. Equation \\ref{eq:thr} shows the completed equation for the perturbation forcing function. $\\omega_{max}$ is the maximum angular velocity of the propeller.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation} \\label{eq:thr}\r\n\t\t\tF_{\\text{perturbation}}(t) = 0.01 \\, \\sin\\big(\\omega_{\\text{max}} \\, T_{\\text{percent}}(t) \\, t \\big) \\, F_{\\text{thrust}}(t)\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tFigure \\ref{fig:purt} shows the perturbation force over time for the optimized UAV model. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{Images/PurtibationVTime.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{Perturbation Vs. Time Chart for Optimized UAV} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:purt}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\r\n\t\r\n\r\n\t\\chapter{Finite Element Analysis} \\label{ch:fea}\r\n\t\r\n\t\t% Description\r\n\t\tThis chapter discusses the architecture of the FEA model designed to be run repeatedly by the optimization algorithm. The finite element model is designed so that it can be easily adapted for different frame models and design variables.\r\n\t\r\n\t\t\\section{Beam Model}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% Describe DFD\r\n\t\t\tA beam model contains the coordinates of the nodes and the element definitions for the beam system. Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_beam} shows a design flow of the process of making the beam model. Nodes each are given a name as well as a set of three-dimensional coordinates within the global coordinate system. Each element is created with these three nodes: the start node, the end node and the k-node. The start and end nodes define the span of the beam. The K-node describes the orientation of the cross section, which is described with a vector. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% Nodes -> elements \r\n\t\t\t% K-nodes \r\n\t\t\t% Section properties \r\n\t\t\t% Material Properties (where do they come from)\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{Images/DFDs/DFDBeamModel.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Creation of the Beam Model} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_beam}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tA set of material properties and cross section properties is associated with each element in the model. The section properties include the type of cross section of the beam element and the dimensions of that cross-section. The two cross-section types used in this thesis are annular and I-beam sections. From these cross section dimensions, the relevant properties are calculated \\cite{StructXannulus}\\cite{StructXibeam} such as: cross-sectional area, second moment of area in all 3 axes, torsional constant, transverse shear deflection constant in both direction on the cross section plane, and the circumscribed radius of the cross section. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThe transverse shear deflection constants are calculated assuming a Poisson's ratio is zero. The equations used for the transverse shear deflection constants are obtained from work by  by Shames and Dym \\cite{transshear}. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThe material properties required for the beam material are the mass density, elastic modulus, Poisson's ratio, tension yield strength. The fatigue strength coefficient, exponent and cycles are required for fatigue analysis. The shear modulus is calculated from the relationship between Poisson's ratio and the elastic modulus. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Beam Element Formulation} \\label{se:beam_element_form}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% Description\t\r\n\t\t\tFor each beam element a stiffness and mass matrix within the local coordinate system must be created. The stiffness matrix uses Timoshenko beam theory so shear deformation effects are taken into account. This creates a more accurate model than Euler–Bernoulli beam which is desired for the use in the transient and dynamic analysis. The local stiffness matrix has 12 degrees of freedom. It has two nodes, each with three displacement and three rotational degrees of freedom. Timoshenko beam theory was selected to account for transverse shear deformation, which allows for more accuracy than Euler–Bernoulli beam theory for the dynamic and transient analyses performed in this study. The stiffness matrix requires the length of the beam. It also requires cross section properties such as area, second moments of area, the torsional constant, and the transverse shear deflection constant. The material properties required are the elastic modulus and shear modulus. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThe mass matrix is a three-dimensional lumped mass matrix. This requires the total mass of the beam, which is calculated by multiplying the length, the density, and the cross section area. The mass matrix also requires the mass moment of inertia tensor in the local coordinate system. The annulus cross section uses the mass moment of inertia tensor for a thick-walled cylindrical tube \\cite{MassMoment}. The I-beam uses mass moment of inertia tensors of three rectangular cuboids \\cite{MassMoment} assembled with parallel-axis theorem. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Boundary Conditions}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tEach solver requires boundary conditions to be applied to solve create the vector of known displacements. Each boundary condition references a node where the boundary condition is applied. The boundary condition requires a boundary condition type, a boundary condition value. The boundary condition type refers to which direction or rotation the node should be constrained in. The boundary condition value is the direction or rotation is set to. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThere is also an option to set a local coordinate system to apply the boundary condition. This requires a vector in the global coordinate system where the x-direction of the boundary condition should be placed in and a k-node vector which determines the y-direction. The directions of the resulting coordinate system is the transformation matrix. If the boundary condition is applied in the global coordinate system, the transformation matrix is set to the identity matrix. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\t\tmodel takes shear deformation effects into account. The mass for each beam is modeled as a 3D lumped mass matrix.\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t- More detail on mass matrix (it includes rotations)\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tBoundary conditions are set relative to a local coordinate system so that the symmetric boundary conditions at the endpoints of the struts. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tStresses and strains are calculated using the formula ??? [cite presentation]\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% Archetecture - BeamElement class\r\n\t\t\tEach beam element within the beam system is associated with a BeamElement object. The local stiffness and mass matrices are calculated with the solve{\\_}stiffness{\\_}matrix method. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% Archetecture - BeamElement class\r\n\t\t\tIntrmediate values for displacments are found using the return{\\_}shape{\\_}functions, return{\\_}shape{\\_}functions{\\_}dynamic, and return{\\_}shape{\\_}functions{\\_}dynamic method. Intermediate values of stress and strain are found using the stress{\\_}strain{\\_}shape{\\_}function method.  \r\n\t\t\t\\end{comment}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{UAV Slice Model} \\label{ss:seg_model}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% Figure of the segment model Stick model\r\n\t\t\t% Describe BCs \r\n\t\t\t% Describe Hub segmentation\r\n\t\t\t% Description\r\n\t\t\tA FEA model of single slice of the UAV frame was created. A representation of this model is shown in Figure \\ref{fig:stickmodel}.  The model is 1/8th of the UAV split along the struts. Dimensional data is taken from the geometry of the UAV described in Section \\ref{se:uav_geo}. All forces applied for the optimization algorithm are applied to the outer node. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThe hub is represented as a series of repeated I-beam cross sections. The width of each I-beam cross-section in the slice model is the width at the centroid of the corresponding trapezoidal area of the hub's top plate. The number of hub segments is input by the user.\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThe center of the UAV hub is fixed in all 6 degrees of freedom. For the constraints at the struts, the local transformation was used to create a symmetric constraint about the plane perpendicular to the struts. Therefore, the only frequency modes analyzed with the slice model are the symmetric modes. However, the symmetric constraint could be changed to asymmetric to take into account asymmetric modes. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tTo generate the beam model of the UAV, the design variables, material properties associated with all the components, and the wingspan and hub height calculations are required.  \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{Images/stickmodel.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{UAV Slice Beam Model Visualization} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:stickmodel}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tOnce the slice model is complete, the individual local stiffness and mass matrices are transformed into the global coordinate system then assembled into a global mass matrix and stiffness matrix for the entire system. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\t% Archetecture - All modules\r\n\t\tThe finite element model is divided into several different modules. The beam{\\_}system module contains the BeamSystem class. The beam{\\_}type module contains BeamType class which stores beam cross section and material definitions. The beam{\\_}element module contains information about each beam element in the BeamElement class. The node module contains the Node class. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tA material property dictionary is stored in matrial{\\_}properties module. Cross section properties are calculated using functions in the cross{\\_}section{\\_}properties module.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t% Archetecture - The Heirachy \r\n\t\tThe BeamSystem class contains list of BeamElement objects and a list of Node objects. The BeamElement object. Each BeamElement object references a BeamType object. The BeamType object stores the material properties retrieved from the material{\\_}module. The cross section porperties are calculated for a given cross section using functions within the cross{\\_}section{\\_}properties module.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t% Archetecture - BeamSystem class\r\n\t\tNodes are added using the add{\\_}node method in the BeamSystem class. The nodes contain a single position in the global coordinate system. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tBeam elements are added to the BeamSystem class using the add{\\_}beam method. The add{\\_}beam method requires a refrence to a start and an end node, a BeamType object and a k node to determine the orientation of the beam's cross-section. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe boundary conditions are added with the add{\\_}boundary{\\_}condition method. The method requires the value of the boundary condition, the boundary condition type (x, y z, x rotation, ect.), and node reference here the boundary condition is applied. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe solve{\\_}FEA method solves the stiffness and mass matrixes for the given system. The solve{\\_}static, solve{\\_}dynamic{\\_}harmonic, and solve{\\_}transient methods find the solution for given forces.   \r\n\t\t\\end{comment}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\\section{Solvers} \\label{se:solvers}\r\n\t\r\n\t\tA FEA model was created with three separate solvers. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe static solver, which is used to calculate the response given a static loading, the dynamic solver, which calculate the response given a force of an oscillating force at a given frequency, and the transient solver which calculates the response given a time-changing force. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Static} \\label{ss:static}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThe static solver calculates the reactions, displacements, and rotations given a specific set of point loads and moments in static equilibrium. The scipy.linalg.solve function is used to solve for unknown displacements. The displacements, rotations, forces, and moments are used to calculate shape functions and stresses for each beam in the state of static stress is calculated using the formulation discussed in section \\ref{ss:seg_model}. Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_static} shows the design flow diagram for the static solver. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.06]{Images/DFDs/DFDStatic.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Static Solver} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_static}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\t\tThe static solver is the solve\\_static method within the BeamSystem class. The method solves the global and local displacements of each beam. The local displacements for each BeamElement object within the BeamSystem object are solved by running the solve\\_local method within each Beam object. This method applies the transformation matrix to the results. The solve\\_shape\\_functions method and find\\_stresses methods are used to find the state of stress at stable equilibrium within each beam. \r\n\t\t\t\\end{comment}\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Dynamic} \\label{ss:dynamic}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThe dynamic solver solves the amplitude of reactions, displacements, and rotations given a set of oscillating loads and moments.  The boundary conditions are static. Each force and moment applied is assumed to have the same frequency and phase for the given solver. The dynamic stiffness matrix is calculated from the stiffness and mass matrices using the formula $[K_d] = [K] - \\omega^2[M]$. Damping is assumed to be 0. $[K_d]$ is the dynamic stiffness matrix.  $[K]$ is the static stiffness matrix and $[M]$ is the mass matrix. $\\omega$ is the frequency of the loads and moments. The scipy.linalg.solve function is used with the dynamic stiffness matrix to solve for unknown displacement amplitudes. Three amplitudes are stored for each direction in three-dimensional space and three amplitudes are stored for each axis of rotation. The oscillating stress amplitude is calculated for each element by substituting the assumed displacements in the stress solver and instead solving for the stress solve for the stress amplitude. Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_dynamic} showes the design flow diagram for the dynamic solver. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.06]{Images/DFDs/DFDDynamic.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Dynamic Solver} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_dynamic}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% self.kuu - (ang_vel ** 2) * self.muu - ang_vel * (self.muu * alpha + self.kuu * beta)\r\n\t\t\t  % As described in page 385 of concepts and applications of finite element analysis. \r\n\t\t\t  \r\n\t\t\t% The dynamic solver is the solve\\_dynamic\\_harmonic method within the BeamSystem class. This method solves for the global and local amplitude for each beam. The local displacements for each beam object within the beam system are solved by running the solve\\_local method within each Beam object. The solve\\_shape\\_functions\\_dynamic method and find\\_stresses\\_dynamic methods are used to find the state of stress at stable equilibrium within each beam. \r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\\subsection{Transient} \\label{ss:transient}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\tThe transient solver finds the reactions, displacements, and rotations over time given a user defined time-varying force and moment. The inputs are a timestep interval (${\\delta}T$), the timestep count ($T_n$), and $\\alpha$ and $\\delta$ as constants for Newmark integration. Each force added to the system is described as a vector of displacements and rotations as function of time. The Newmark integration method is used to estimate the displacement, rotations, and reactions at each timestep. There is a constant duration between each timestep which is determined by the timestep interval. The stresses are calculated for each individual timestep.  Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_transient} shows the design flow diagram for the transient solver. \r\n\t\r\n\t\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.06]{Images/DFDs/DFDTransient.png}\r\n\t\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Transient Solver} \r\n\t\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_trans}\r\n\t\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t% The transient solver is initiated with the initialize\\_dynamic\\_transient method within the BeamSystem class. The solve\\_dynamic\\_transient method within the BeamSystem class solves for the global and local reactions, displacements, and rotations for each timestep. Within the BeamElement class, the solve\\_shape\\_functions\\_transient and find\\_stresses\\_transient methods are used to find the stresses and strains for each beam at each timestep. \r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\\section{Beam Stresses}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tOnce the global stresses are calculated, they are transformed back into the local coordinate space so that the shape functions can be calculated. The shape functions used are three dimensional and $C^1$ continuous. \\cite{feabook} The shape functions require a specific value of x, representing the distance along the beam in local coordinates. The shape functions allow for displacement and rotations to be interpolated at any point within the beam. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tUsing the shape functions, the bending stress can be calculated at any point along the beam. The formula used is $\\sigma_b(x)=-hE\\frac{d\\theta(x)}{dx}$. $\\sigma_b(x)$ is the maximum bending stress in the beam in a given principle bending axis. $\\frac{d\\theta(x)}{dx}$ Is the derivative of rotation along the beam axis obtained from the shape functions. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t$E$ is the Young's Modulus. $h$ is the radius of circumscribed cross section. This causes an overestimation of the stress and was selected to simplify the calculation. This causes the stress constraint to be more stringent than reality. The bending stress is calculated for both bending axes and the resulting maximum stress is taken by taking Euclidean norm of both vectors. \r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\\section{Mass Analysis} \\label{se:mass}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe total mass of the frame ($m_{frame}$) of the UAV was calculated geometrically, by calculating the volume of each beam and multiplying it by the density of the associated material. The mass of each battery and the payload was added to obtain the total mass of the vehicle ($m_{total}$). \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe mass moment of inertia tensor for the UAV frame is calculated. This is used in the objective function of the optimization problem. The calculation only takes into account the mass moment of the UAV frame, and not the mass moment of the batteries or the payload. % Ask about this?? \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe mass moment of the system is calculated by summing the mass moment of each beam in the finite element model. The calculation for mass moment has the hub subdivided into individual I-beams. The principal moments of inertia are calculated by taking the eigenvalues of the tensor. The eigenvalues are solved using np.linalg.eig.\r\n\t \r\n\t\r\n\t\\chapter{Optimization} \\label{se:optimization}\r\n\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\tdesign variables: \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{itemize}\r\n\t\t\t\\item $d_a$: arm diameter,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $t_a$: arm thickness,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $d_s$: strut diameter,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $t_s$: strut thickness,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $r_s$: strut distance from center,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $t_f$: hub flange thickness,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $t_w$: hub web thickness \r\n\t\t\t\\item $h_r$: hub radius\r\n\t\t\\end{itemize}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tderived variables:\r\n\t\t\\begin{itemize}\r\n\t\t\t\\item $d_{tip}$: Deflection at tip\r\n\t\t\t\\item $\\sigma_{max}$: Maximum stress from bending \r\n\t\t\t\\item $f_{amp}$: Amplitude of displacement at the tip of the uav arm\r\n\t\t\t\\item $D$: Damage per mission\r\n\t\t\t\\item $ E_{used}$: Energy used over the duration of the mission\r\n\t\t\t\\item $ E_{total}$: Energy used over th duration of the mission\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\\end{itemize}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tdesign parameters:\r\n\t\t\\begin{itemize}\r\n\t\t\t\\item $d_{allowable}$: Deflection allowable\r\n\t\t\t\\item $\\sigma_{allowable}$: Stress allowable from bending \r\n\t\t\t\\item $R_{allowable}$: Allowable amplitude displacement ratio\r\n\t\t\t\\item $n_{missions} $: number of missions before damage\r\n\t\t\\end{itemize}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t- Optimization algorithm used is SLSQP method using 's minimization function.\r\n\t\t \\end{comment}\r\n\t\t \r\n\t\t This chapter describes how the optimization problem is formulated and how the optimization architecture was designed. It describes the boundary conditions, optimization function and constraints. \r\n\t\t \t\t \r\n\t\t The optimization problem is formulated as follows:\r\n\t\t \r\n\t\t \\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t \t\\min J(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w)\r\n\t\t \t\\label{eq:opt_objective}\r\n\t\t \\end{equation}\r\n\t\t \r\n\t\t subject to constraints:\r\n\t\t \r\n\t\t \\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t \tg_j(\\mathbf{x}) \\le 0, \\quad j = 1, 2, \\dots, J\r\n\t\t \t\\label{eq:opt_constraints}\r\n\t\t \\end{equation}\r\n\t\t \r\n\t\t \\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t \tx_i^L \\le x_i \\le x_i^U, \\quad i = 1, 2, \\dots, N\r\n\t\t \t\\label{eq:opt_bounds}\r\n\t\t \\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe optimization function uses the SLSQP algorithm. This is run with scipy.optimize.minimize algorithm's SLSQP method. The tolerance is set to 1e-6. Optimization is considered complete only if the results were terminated successfully.To save on computation time, constraints can be made active or inactive by the solver, as well as setting design variables to constants if the boundaries are active. The next sections describe the formulation of the objective function, the boundary conditions, and the constraints. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\\subsection{Objective Function} \\label{ss:desvar}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe objective function is formulated in order to both minimize the mass and mass moment of inertia of the UAV frame. The objective function is described in Equation \\ref{eq:objective_function}. $M_{\\text{frame}}$ is the mass of the frame given a set of design variables. $I_{\\text{frame}}$ is the mass moment of inertia of the frame for a given set of design variables. $M_i$ is a constant which is equal the mass of the frame at the initial guess. $I_i$ is a constant which is equal the mass of the frame at the initial guess. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t\tJ(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w) =\r\n\t\t\t\\frac{M_{\\text{frame}}(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w)}{M_i}\r\n\t\t\t+\r\n\t\t\t\\frac{I_{\\text{frame}}(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w)}{I_i}\r\n\t\t\t\\label{eq:objective_function}\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe design flow diagram showing how the objective function is calculated is shown in Figure \\ref{dfd_objective}. The dimensions of the drone are calculated using a set of the design variables and parameters. The mass is calculated geometrically from the geometry and material properties. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe beam model of the full UAV with the segmented hub from the slice model described in Subsection \\ref{ss:seg_model} is created. The moment of inertia for this beam model is calculated. The centroid and the mass moment of inertia tensor for each beam is taken. The mass moments of inertia tensors are transformed into the global coordinate system, then parallel axis theorem is applied to find the mass moment of inertia of the entire system. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{Images/DFDs/DFDObjective.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Objective Function} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_objective}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t% The mass is calculated in the DroneMass class within the drone{\\_}mass module. The DroneMass class takes a DroneGeometry object as an input. It uses the material information, cross-section properties, and Beam and hub coordinates within the DroneGeometry object to calculate the mass of the UAV frame. \r\n\t\r\n\t\\subsection{Constraints} \\label{ss:consts}\r\n\t\r\n\tThis section describes all the constraints applied to the UAV model. There are boundary conditions on all the design variables, a displacement constraint to limit the maximum displacement while the UAV is in hover, a constraint to ensure the stress is below yield strength, a frequency constraint to prevent vibration, an energy constraint to ensure that the UAV has enough energy to complete the mission, and a fatigue constraint to prevent long-term damage to the vehicle. \r\n\t\r\n\t\\subsubsection{Boundaries} \\label{sss:Boundss}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tEach design variable has lower and upper limit applied to it. Arm diameter\r\n\t\tEquations \\ref{eq:bound_da} - \\ref{eq:bound_hr} describe each boundary condition. The arm diameter ($d_a$), arm thickness ($t_a$), strut diameter ($d_s$), strut thickness ($t_s$) boundary conditions were obtained through the minimum and maximum pipe diameter and thickness for braided carbon fiber found on AllRED's Dragonplate Website. \\cite{DragonPlate}. The hub flange thickness ($t_f$) and hub web thickness ($t_w$) boundary conditions were taken from the minimum and maximum thickness of carbon fiber sheets. The maximum boundary conditions on hub radius ($h_r$) and strut distance from the center ($r_s$) are the distance from the center of the UAV to the propellers so the struts and hub stay within the frame. The minimum constraint of 197.5mm for hub radius ($h_r$) and strut distance ($r_s$) is the minimum hub radius for a circle to circumscribe both batteries with their given positions and dimensions.  \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tEach design variable is bounded by a lower and upper limit. The bounds for the arm and strut diameters and thicknesses are taken from the minimum and maximum available sizes for braided carbon fiber pipe on AllRED’s DragonPlate website \\cite{DragonPlate}. The hub flange and web thickness bounds are based on the minimum and maximum thicknesses available for carbon fiber sheets. The maximum bounds for the hub radius ($h_r$) and strut distance ($r_s$) correspond to the distance from the UAV center to the propellers so that the frame remains within the propeller circle. The minimum constraint of $197.5\\,$mm for $h_r$ and $r_s$ is the minimum radius required for a circle to circumscribe both batteries with their given dimensions and positions.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\t\t\\item $d_a$: arm diameter,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $t_a$: arm thickness,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $d_s$: strut diameter,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $t_s$: strut thickness,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $r_s$: strut distance from center,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $t_f$: hub flange thickness,\r\n\t\t\t\\item $t_w$: hub web thickness \r\n\t\t\t\\item $h_r$: hub radius\r\n\t\t\\end{comment}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{align}\r\n\t\t\t9\\,\\text{mm} &\\le d_a \\le 50.0\\,\\text{mm} \\label{eq:bound_da}\\\\\r\n\t\t\t0.254\\,\\text{mm} &\\le t_a \\le 3.175\\,\\text{mm} \\label{eq:bound_ta}\\\\\r\n\t\t\t9\\,\\text{mm} &\\le d_s \\le 50.0\\,\\text{mm} \\label{eq:bound_ds}\\\\\r\n\t\t\t0.254\\,\\text{mm} &\\le t_s \\le 3.175\\,\\text{mm} \\label{eq:bound_ts}\\\\\r\n\t\t\t197.5\\,\\text{mm} &\\le r_s \\le 1127.3\\,\\text{mm} \\label{eq:bound_rs}\\\\\r\n\t\t\t0.7\\,\\text{mm} &\\le t_f \\le 12.7\\,\\text{mm} \\label{eq:bound_tf}\\\\\r\n\t\t\t0.7\\,\\text{mm} &\\le t_w \\le 12.7\\,\\text{mm} \\label{eq:bound_tw}\\\\\r\n\t\t\t197.5\\,\\text{mm} &\\le h_r \\le 1127.3\\,\\text{mm} \\label{eq:bound_hr}\r\n\t\t\\end{align}\r\n\t\r\n\t\\subsubsection{Displacement and Stress} \\label{sss:displacement}\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\tConstraints on the maximum allowable displacement and stress while hovering are created. The design flow diagram of the process of creating these constraints is shown in Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_disp}. The drone geometry is the set of all relevant coordinates, material properties, and cross section geometry for the UAV. From this geometry data the mass of the frame is calculated. The mass of the frame is added to the mass of both batteries and the payload to obtain the total mass. This is divided by the number of blades and multiplied by gravity to obtain the equivalent force required for hover. This force points upward and is on the outer node of the segment model. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe beam model is the UAV slice model discussed in Subsection \\ref{ss:seg_model}. The static solver discussed in Subsection \\ref{ss:static} was used to calculate the maximum displacement and the maximum stress within the beam model. The displacement used is the highest magnitude displacement within the beam model ($d_{max}$). The stress is the maximum bending stress within the beam model ($\\sigma_{max}$). \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{Images/DFDs/DFDDisp.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Creation of the Displacement and Stress Constraints} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_disp}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe formula for the constraint is shown in \\ref{eq:opt_disp}. $d_{allowable}$ is the maximum allowable displacement.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t \\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t\tg_1(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w) = d_{\\max} - d_{\\text{allowable}} \\le 0\r\n\t\t\t\\label{eq:opt_disp}\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t \t$\\sigma_{yeild}$ is the tension yield strength of the material. $\\text{FOS}$ is the factor of safety associated with the stress constraint. Factor of safety is a parameter of the analysis. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t\tg_2(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w) = \\sigma_{\\max} - \\frac{\\sigma_{\\text{yield}}}{\\text{FOS}} \\le 0\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\label{eq:opt_stress}\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\\subsubsection{Frequency} \\label{sss:frquency}\r\n\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe objective of the frequency constraint is to ensure that the frequency experienced by the propeller is not near the natural frequency of the vehicle. This is accomplished by calculating the maximum admittance over a range of values.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe design flow diagram for the frequency is shown in Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_freq}. First, the beam model of the UAV slice discussed in Subsection \\ref{ss:seg_model} is created. This beam model is used for both the dynamic and static solvers. The same force is applied to the static model which has a magnitude equal to the amplitude of the force on dynamic model. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe dynamic model is run repeatedly with frequencies over a range of values. This range is calculated by from converting finding the maximum and minimum values for thrust on the thrust curve calculated in Subsection \\ref{ss:thrust}, then converting those values to minimum and maximum frequency using Equation \\ref{eq:ang}.\r\n\t\tThe rev-up and rev-down sections are not considered for the thrust analysis. The maximum displacement amplitude between all run dynamic models ($d_amplitude$) is found. The maximum displacement in the static model was also found ($d_{static}$). Equation \\ref{eq:opt_freq} is the formulation of the constraint.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t\tg_3(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w)\r\n\t\t\t= \\frac{d_{\\text{amplitude}}}{d_{\\text{static}}} - R_{\\text{allowable}}\r\n\t\t\t\\le 0\r\n\t\t\t\\label{eq:opt_freq}\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{Images/DFDs/DFDFrequency.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Transient Solver} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_freq}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\\subsubsection{Damage} \\label{sss:damage}\r\n\t\r\n\t\tThe damage constraint is imposed in order to limit damage caused by the vibrations in the vehicle. The design flow diagram of the is shown in Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_damage}. The mass of the drone is calculated geometrically using model discussed in Section \\ref{se:uav_geo}. The mass of the batteries and payload are taken to find the total mass ($m_total$). The projected surface area of the UAV is calculated. This is the sum of the area of the top surface of the UAV hub and the projected surface area of the UAV arms and struts. The mission profile, total mass, and surface area are used to calculate the thrust function shown in Equation \\ref{eq:thrust}. The thrust function is transferred to a angular frequency function by applying Equation \\ref{eq:ang}. The thrust function is then used to calculate the perturbation force described by Equation \\ref{eq:thr}. The beam model used is the slice model discussed in Subsection \\ref{ss:seg_model}. Both the perturbation and thrust are upward forces and are applied to the outer node of the slice model. Both forces are divided by eight due to only applying to one slice of the model. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe number of time-steps for the transient solver is set by a constant set by the user. The constants used for Newmark-beta interrogation are $\\beta = 0.25$ and $\\gamma = 0.5$. The results for the bending stress are taken for each timestep at the point of maximum bending stress. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tOnce the stress function is obtained, the rainflow counting algorithm is applied to calculate the damage. The Rainflow module in the PyLife library is used and it uses the four-point Rainflow-counting algorithm to transfer the time-varying load into sets of constant amplitude stress reversals. This is used with the Woehler curve data to calculate the total damage accumulated over the mission. This is done using the LoadCollective class in the PyLife library. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tOne limitation of this analysis is that material data is fatigue material data is limited to aluminum as fatigue data for the carbon fiber composite has not been obtained as that is outside the scope of this thesis. Equation \\ref{eq:opt_damage} describes the constraint. $D$ is the damage taken in a single mission. $n_\\text{missions}$ is the number of missions the UAV should run before failure.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t\tg_4(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w)\r\n\t\t\t= D \\, n_{\\text{missions}} - 1\r\n\t\t\t\\le 0\r\n\t\t\t\\label{eq:opt_damage}\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.06]{Images/DFDs/DFDDamage.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Transient Solver} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_damage}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\\subsubsection{Energy} \\label{sss:energy}\r\n\t\r\n\t\tThe objective of this constraint is to keep battery energy above desired levels for the duration of the mission. The design flow diagram for the energy constraint is shown in Figure \\ref{fig:dfd_energy}. The energy constraint uses the energy consumption calculation in Subsection \\ref{ss:energy_consumption_calc} to determine the total energy consumed during the mission. $E_{used}$ is the total energy used during the mission, it is equal to $E_{Capacity} - E_{Remaining}(t)$ where t is the full duration of the power mission profile. $E_{Capacity}$ is the total storage of the battery. $E_{\\%remaining}$ is a parameter which is equal to the amount of percent energy remaining desired after landing. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{equation}\r\n\t\t\tg_5(d_a,t_a,d_s,t_s,r_s,t_f,t_w)\r\n\t\t\t= \\frac{E_{\\text{used}}}{E_{\\text{capacity}}} + E_{\\%\\text{remaining}} - 1 \r\n\t\t\t\\le 0\r\n\t\t\t\\label{eq:opt_energy}\r\n\t\t\\end{equation}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{figure}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\\centering\r\n\t\t\t\\includegraphics[scale=0.1]{Images/DFDs/DFDEnergy.png}\r\n\t\t\t\\caption{DFD of the Energy Constraint Calculation} \r\n\t\t\t\\label{fig:dfd_energy}\r\n\t\t\\end{figure}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\tself.active_constraints = self.parameters_defaults.copy()\r\n\t\tself.parameters = self.parameters_defaults.copy()\r\n\t\tself.design_variables_initial_guess = self.design_variables_initial_guess_defaults.copy()\r\n\t\tself.constraints_constants = self.constraints_constants_defaults.copy()\r\n\t\tself.simulation_settings = self.simulation_settings_defaults.copy()\r\n\t\tself.boundaries = self.boundaries_defaults.copy()\r\n\t\tself.drone_geometry = 0\r\n\t\tself.fixed_design_variables = self.fixed_design_variables_defaults.copy()\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe optimization module contains the Optimizer class. This class runs code related to the optimization algorithm. The primary inputs are a dictionary of active constraints, parameters, an initial guess, parameters related to the fidelity of the simulation, boundary conditions, and a dictionary of design variables that are fixed. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe active{\\_}constraints dictionary has a boolean associated with each constraint. These allow the user to turn off constraints if they are inactive to save on computation time. The parameters dictionary contains design parameters. This includes number of blades, propeller radius, propeller spacing, payload, air density, drag coefficient, maximum propeller rotations per minute, battery mass, battery dimensions, battery volts, battery milliamp hours, rev up time, rev down time, gravity, and hub height. These can all be changed easily to test various UAV designs and flight conditions.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe parameters related to the fidelity of the simulation are the number of I-beams describing the hub, the number of time-steps in the transient load simulation, and the number of time-steps for integration for the energy usage calculation. The boundary conditions are a dictionary with each constraint associated with a touple expressing the range of acceptable values. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe dictionary of fixed design variables has each constraint either associated with a number, or is set to false. If set to a number the design variable is fixed and is not analyzed by the optimizer. This is done after it is determined that the boundary condition is the active constraint so the design variable does not need to be considered. Fixing the design variables saves on computation time. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tFirst the independent{\\_}properties{\\_}calc method is run in order to perform necessary calculations not associated with any design varaibles. This includes the creating the mission profile, BatterySystem, and Battery objects. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe mass method calculates the mass of the UAV given a set of design variables. The constraint{\\_}calculation method runs a set of constraints given a set of design variables and active constraints. The formulation of eeach constraint is set up so that if the result of the constraint is above 1, the constraint is violated.\r\n\t\t  \r\n\t\tThe run\\_opt method runs the optimizer. The Scypi library's scipy.optimize.minimize is used with the the 'SLSQP' method. Max iterations are set to 10000 and the error tolerance is set to 1e-18. The function outputs the resulting design variables as well as the result of the objective function.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tTest\\_results method runs all constraints to ensure that an optimized result does not violate any constraints that were set to inactive. \r\n\t\t\\end{comment}\r\n\t\\chapter{Results} \\label{ch:results}\r\n\t\t\\section{Something}\r\n\t\t\t- My thing is very flexible and practical. Rapid design testing etc. etc. \r\n\t\t\t- I will show how practical it is by showing how easy it is to modify base on results \r\n\t\t\t- Initially started with a deflection constraint of 5\\% deflection per span. \r\n\t\t\t- Determined it was too low (based on transient results)\r\n\t\t\t- Moved to 1\\% Deflection per space\r\n\t\t\t- Energy constraint is based on mass so this shows the mass deflection tradeoff.\r\n\t\t\t- This was determined to be too high because the transient analysis \r\n\t\t\t\t- The deflection was higher than the static analysis\r\n\t\t\t\t- This is expected. \r\n\t\t\t \r\n\t\t\\section{Initial Design}\r\n\t\t\t\\subsection{Initial Guess}\r\n\t\t\t\topt.design_variables_initial_guess = {\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\"arm_diameter\": 50.0,\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\"arm_thickness\": 0.6,\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\"strut_diameter\": 50.0,\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\"strut_thickness\": 0.6,\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\"strut_distance\": 475,\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\"hub_radius\": 250,\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\"hub_flange_thickness\": 0.7,\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\"hub_web_thickness\": 0.7\r\n\t\t\t\t}\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\subsection{resulting DVS}\r\n\t\t\t\tdesign variables\r\n\t\t\t\tarm_diameter: 50.0\r\n\t\t\t\tarm_thickness: 0.2540000000000008\r\n\t\t\t\tstrut_diameter: 50.0\r\n\t\t\t\tstrut_thickness: 0.2540000000000002\r\n\t\t\t\tstrut_distance: 454.2624850257739\r\n\t\t\t\thub_radius: 250\r\n\t\t\t\thub_flange_thickness: 0.7\r\n\t\t\t\thub_web_thickness: 0.7\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\tInitial Guess\r\n\t\t\t\tFrame mass: 1.7057551016244064 kg\r\n\t\t\t\tFrame mass moment of inertia: 0.34047109121624436  kg*m^2\r\n\t\t\t\tObjective: 0.9384796481845028\r\n\t\t\t\tOptimized\r\n\t\t\t\tFrame mass: 1.053752071416452 kg\r\n\t\t\t\tFrame mass moment of inertia: 0.14823664329358247 kg*m^2\r\n\t\t\t\tObjective: 0.4850405167872752\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\t-Sensitivity analysis \r\n\t\t\t\t- The value of the design variables which did not converge to the boundary conditions were varied by +-0.1\\% of their values to test the optimal solution.   \r\n\t\t\t\t\\begin{table}[htb]\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\t\\end{table}\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\subsection{Constraints}\r\n\t\t\t\tdeflection: 1.0000000000000078\r\n\t\t\t\tstress: 0.8763661383057956\r\n\t\t\t\tdisplacement amplitude: 0.19538100852334553\r\n\t\t\t\tenergy: 0.8892813083366702\r\n\t\t\t\tdamage: 0.0\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\tDamage is 0 because the fatigue is minor and it uses data for aluminum which is stronger than the carbon fiber composites. \r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\tDeflection is the active constraint.\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t- The initiales guess\twere based off of measurements from the original UAV design. \r\n\t\t\t- arm diameter, strut diameter, hub radius, flange thickness, and web thickness were all found to converge to boundary conditions\r\n\t\t\\section{Revised Design }\r\n\t\t\t\\section{Why}\r\n\t\t\t\t- 50 too high. \r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\t- Initially started with a deflection constraint of 5\\% deflection per span. \r\n\t\t\t\t- Moved to 1\\% Deflection per space\r\n\t\t\t\t- Energy constraint is based on mass so this shows the mass deflection tradeoff.\r\n\t\t\t\t- This was determined to be too high because the transient analysis \r\n\t\t\t\t- The deflection was higher than the static analysis\r\n\t\t\t\t- This is expected. \r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\\section{dvs}\r\n\t\t\t\t\r\n\t\t% initial_guess = {\r\n\t\t%\t\"arm_diameter\": 50.0,\r\n\t\t%\t\"arm_thickness\": 0.6,\r\n\t\t%\t\"strut_diameter\": 50.0,\r\n\t\t%\t\"strut_thickness\": 0.6,\r\n\t\t%\t\"strut_distance\": 425,\r\n\t\t%\t\"hub_radius\": 100.0,\r\n\t\t%\t\"hub_flange_thickness\": 0.7,\r\n\t\t%\t\"hub_web_thickness\": 0.7\r\n\t\t% }\t\r\n\t\t\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\tdesign variables\r\n\t\tarm_diameter: 50.0\r\n\t\tarm_thickness: 0.30233957405287726\r\n\t\tstrut_diameter: 50.0\r\n\t\tstrut_thickness: 0.3609053941590715\r\n\t\tstrut_distance: 556.4454900912388\r\n\t\thub_radius: 100.0\r\n\t\thub_flange_thickness: 0.7\r\n\t\thub_web_thickness: 0.7\r\n\t\tconstraints\r\n\t\tdeflection: 0.9999999999999993\r\n\t\tstress: 0.977859849282712\r\n\t\tdisplacement amplitude: 0.29064885956701497\r\n\t\tenergy: 0.3260612162830091\r\n\t\tdamage: 0.0\r\n\t\tmass w/ payload: 0.8250115057269486 kg\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\\end{comment}\r\n\t\t\r\n\t% \\chapter{Discussion} \\label{ch:discussion}\r\n\t\\chapter{Conclusions} \\label{ch:conclusions}\r\n\t\t- The design couldbe improved. \r\n\t\t- Work can be done. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\\section{Further Work} \\label{se:further_work}\r\n\t\tTakee into account UAV tilt\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tObejective function with  momeent of inertia. \r\n\t\r\n\t\tThe UAV mesh can be refined\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tThe hub model is an simplification, as it can not fit the entire battery within the bounds of hub. Further work can be done to create a more detailed model. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\tAsymmetric modes should be considered. \r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\\chapter{Summary} \\label{ch:summary}\r\n\t\r\n\t% This is the standard bibtex file. Do not include the .bib extension in <bib_file_name>.\r\n\t% Uncomment the following lines to include your bibliography: \r\n\t\\bibliography{Thesis_refs}\r\n\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}   \r\n\t\r\n\t% This formats the chapter name to appendix to properly define the headers:\r\n\t\\appendix\r\n\t\r\n\t% Add your appendices here. You must leave the appendices enclosed in the appendices environment in order for the table of contents to be correct.\r\n\t\\begin{appendices}\r\n\t\t\\chapter{First Appendix} \\label{app:appendix_one}\r\n\t\t\\section{Section one} \\label{ase:app_one_sect_1}\r\n\t\t\\section{Section two} \\label{ase:app_one_sect_2}\r\n\t\t\\chapter{Second Appendix} \\label{app:appendix_two}\r\n\t\\end{appendices}\r\n\t\r\n\\end{document}\r\n\r\n\\begin{comment}\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\t1. Introduction\r\n\ta. Overview\r\n\tb. Goals and objectives\r\n\tc. Scope\r\n\td. thesis outline\r\n\t2. Literature Review\r\n\t3. Methodology\r\n\ta. UAV design\r\n\ti. UAV geometry \r\n\ta) octocopter, batteries, payload\r\n\ti. free body diagram\r\n\tb) wingspan calculation\r\n\tc) describe drone_geometry module\r\n\tii. mission profile design\r\n\t`\ta) define mission profile segments \r\n\tb) demonstrate kinematic constraints used\r\n\tc) results\r\n\t\r\n\tiii. battery and propeller selection\r\n\t\r\n\tc. finite element model\r\n\ti. description of the UAV segment model\r\n\ta. describe the boundary conditions, materials ect. \r\n\tii. beam element formulation\r\n\ta) timeshenko beams\r\n\tb) boundary conditions\r\n\tc) mass matrices\r\n\tiii. solvers\r\n\ta) static \r\n\ti. outputs stress strain\r\n\ta) stress and stain plots of th UAV given the payload\r\n\tb) dynamic \r\n\ti. inputs \r\n\ta) description of the loads\r\n\tii. outputs amplitude of the response given a force strain, displacement, reactions, ect.\r\n\tc) transient\r\n\ti. input \r\n\ta) description of the loads\r\n\tii. outputs reactions and displacements over time. Assumes no damping.\r\n\tiv. Results \r\n\t\r\n\td. optimization \r\n\ta. objective function: mass calculation\r\n\t\r\n\tb. design variables\r\n\ti. figure showing design variable measurements\r\n\tii. upper and lower bounds \r\n\t\r\n\tc. constraints\r\n\ti. displacement\r\n\ta) describe force\r\n\tb) static solver\r\n\tii. stress (treating like black aluminum)\r\n\ta) describe force\r\n\tb) static solver\r\n\tiii. frequency\r\n\ta) constraint formula \t\r\n\tb) describe forcing function \r\n\tc) dynamic solver\r\n\tiv. damage\r\n\ta) constraint formula \t\r\n\tb) describe forcing function \r\n\tc) transient solver\r\n\tv. energy\r\n\ta) constraint formula \r\n\tb) show energy consumption calculation\r\n\t\r\n\td. results \r\n\tImagesImages\r\n\ta) plots of the design space\r\n\tb) show optimized value and compare it to initial\r\n\t5. Summary\t\t\t\t\r\n\t6. Conclusions\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\\end{comment}\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n%****************************************************************************\r\n% Below are some general suggestions for writing your dissertation:\r\n%\r\n% 1. Label everything with a meaningful prefix so that you\r\n%    can refer back to sections, tables, figures, equations, etc.\r\n%    Usage \\label{<prefix>:<label_name>} where some suggested\r\n%    prefixes are:\r\n%\t\t\tch: Chapter\r\n%     \t\tse: Section\r\n%     \t\tss: Subsection\r\n%     \t\tsss: Sub-subsection\r\n%\t\t\tapp: Appendix\r\n%     \t\tase: Appendix section\r\n%     \t\ttab: Tables\r\n%     \t\tfig: Figures\r\n%     \t\tsfig: Sub-figures\r\n%     \t\teq: Equations\r\n%\r\n% 2. The VTthesis class provides for natbib citations. You should upload\r\n%\t one or more *.bib bibtex files. Suppose you have two bib files: some_refs.bib and \r\n%    other_refs.bib.  Then your bibliography line to include them\r\n%    will be:\r\n%      \\bib